  "prompt": "Installers usually select several extras at once, but Marker.evaluate() only accepts one string for `extra`, so an installer has to evaluate a marker once per extra. That gets negated markers wrong: with extras {\"gpu\", \"cpu\"} selected, `extra != \"gpu\"` is True for \"cpu\" although \"gpu\" is selected (see pypa/pip#14139). Allow the `extra` environment value to be a set of extra names and evaluate the marker against the whole set.\n\nDocumentation for the user guide (docs/markers.rst), \"Evaluating `extra` markers against a set of extras\":\n\n> Installers usually select several extras at once. Passing a set of extra names as the `extra` environment value evaluates the marker against the whole set. Logical operators work as you'd expect. The dependency specifier specification says `extra != \"name\"` should not be used in published metadata but allows arbitrary handling; this is handled logically if encountered. Any operator other than `==` and `!=` on `extra` raises UndefinedComparison in this mode.\n> ```\n> >>> marker = Marker('extra == \"gpu\"')\n> >>> marker.evaluate(environment={\"extra\": {\"gpu\", \"docs\"}})\n> True\n> >>> both = Marker('extra == \"gpu\" and extra == \"docs\"')\n> >>> both.evaluate(environment={\"extra\": {\"gpu\", \"docs\"}})\n> True\n> >>> both.evaluate(environment={\"extra\": {\"gpu\"}})\n> False\n> >>> marker.evaluate(environment={\"extra\": set()})   # an empty set behaves like no selected extra\n> False\n> ```\n> prepare_environment accepts a set of extras too. A set given directly to Marker.evaluate_prepared must hold normalized names.\n\n## Contract (also checked)\n- Marker.evaluate accepts any non-string iterable of names as `extra` (set, frozenset, list, tuple). Names are normalized like single extras (canonicalize_name), on both sides: `extra == \"PDF_Support\"` matches {\"pdf.support\"}.\n- `==` means the name is in the selection, `!=` that it is not; the literal may be on either side (`\"gpu\" == extra`). An empty selection behaves like extra == \"\": `extra == \"\"` is True for set(), False for {\"gpu\"}; `extra != \"gpu\"` is True for set().\n- With a set, every other operator on `extra` raises UndefinedComparison: ~=, <, >=, `\"gpu\" in extra`, `\"gpu\" not in extra`, `extra in \"gpu,docs\"`, `extra not in \"gpu\"`.\n- prepare_environment({\"extra\": {\"GPU\", \"docs\"}})[\"extra\"] == frozenset({\"gpu\", \"docs\"}); an empty set (or None, or \"\") becomes \"\". evaluate_prepared accepts a set or frozenset of already normalized names, evaluated the same way as evaluate.\n- Unchanged: a single string `extra` behaves as before, extra=None in evaluate is treated as \"\", and evaluate_prepared with extra=None still raises UndefinedComparison (\"Marker 'extra' must be a string\").\n\nImplement this in the library code under src/, keep everything else working, and add tests under tests/. Run the test suite with: PYTHONPATH=src /Users/Shared/swarmscope-py/bin/python -m pytest -q tests\n",
